{"assertion":{"kind":"theoretical","text":"Erdős Problem #685: declared status 'open'. Formalized: no. Let $\\epsilon>0$ and $n$ be large depending on $\\epsilon$. Is it true that for all $n^\\epsilon<k\\leq n^{1-\\epsilon}$ the number of distinct prime divisors of $\\binom{n}{k}$ is\\[(1+o(1))k\\sum_{k<p<n}\\frac{1}{p}?\\]Or perhaps even when $k \\geq (\\log n)^c$? Current best: It is trivial that the number of prime factors is\\[>\\frac{\\log \\binom{n}{k}}{\\log n},\\]and this inequality becomes (asymptotic) equality if $k>n^{1-o(1)}$. Prize: no. Tags: binomial coefficients, number theory, primes."},"claim_id":"vcl_c66966bc7b5533d9f38321a28c22dcc93c320a00539627e76989913e971d1c2f","conditions":[],"created_at":"2026-06-16T00:00:00Z","evidence":[],"extensions":{"vela.legacy-finding.v1":{"annotations":[],"attachments":[],"confidence":{"basis":"external catalogue record","extraction_confidence":1.0,"kind":"frontier_epistemic","method":"llm_initial","score":0.5},"flags":{"contested":false,"declining":false,"gap":false,"gravity_well":false,"negative_space":false,"retracted":false},"legacy_conditions":{"duration":null,"text":""},"legacy_evidence":{"evidence_spans":[],"method":"erdos_deep","model_system":"n/a","replicated":false,"replication_count":null,"type":"theoretical"},"legacy_links":[],"legacy_provenance":{"authors":[],"doi":null,"extraction":{"extracted_at":"","extractor_version":"","method":"database_import","model":null,"model_version":null},"review":null,"source_type":"database_record","title":"erdos_deep:685","year":null},"previous_version":null,"source_schema":"https://vela.science/schema/finding-bundle/v0.10.0","source_version":1,"updated":null}},"imported_from":{"era":"finding_v0_10","object_id":"vf_b83f7ffefc816eb4","object_root":"sha256:03dc16fb0c5cead61cbb55124ee7c75dc7797f678fbd5fc518b132ea66b168df","predecessor_commit":"c236b4fdedb25e0d726e24e2780d50962e4844f7"},"provenance":[{"kind":"database_record","title":"erdos_deep:685"}],"relations":[],"revision